  "Number": 4,
  "Question": "If \\(x\\lt0,\\) then which of the following must be positive?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{x}{|x|}\\)",
    "B": "\\(-x^2\\)",
    "C": "\\(-2^x\\)",
    "D": "\\(-x^{-1}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\sqrt[3]{x}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Because \\(x<0\\), we have \\(x\\ne0\\), and its reciprocal is negative. The negative-exponent rule gives\n\\[\n-x^{-1}=-\\frac1x>0.\n\\]\nThus choice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\) must be positive.\n\nFor completeness, the other choices are negative. Since \\(|x|=-x\\), we have \\(x/|x|=-1\\). Also, \\(-x^2=-(x^2)<0\\), and \\(-2^x=-(2^x)<0\\) because a positive base raised to a real power is positive. Finally, the real cube root of a negative number is negative. Hence \\(\\boxed{\\mathrm{D}}\\) is the unique answer.",
